WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389
Small-to-big: retrieve small, read big.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:09.325
The precision-versus-context tension has a structural fix: use different sizes for matching and for reading.

3
00:00:09.605 --> 00:00:30.043
In parent and child retrieval (also called small-to-big), you split each document into large parent chunks (a full section, say 1,000 tokens), split each parent into small child chunks (100 to 300 tokens), embed only the children, and keep a pointer from each child to its parent.

4
00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:39.411
At query time you match against the sharp, small children, then hand the model the parents they point to, deduplicated, so the model reads full context.

5
00:00:39.691 --> 00:00:44.171
LlamaIndex documents this as recursive retrieval with node references.

6
00:00:44.451 --> 00:00:59.704
Its example splits a paper into 1,024-token parents with children of 128, 256, and 512 tokens, and on its own generated question set reports hit rate rising from 0.

7
00:00:59.984 --> 00:01:01.925
778 to 0.

8
00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:05.085
897 and MRR from 0.

9
00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:07.328
563 to 0.

10
00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:10.467
691 over the plain baseline.

11
00:01:10.747 --> 00:01:16.912
That is one document and one question set, so read it as “worth trying,” not as a general result.

12
00:01:17.192 --> 00:01:24.509
A variant attaches a generated summary and generated questions to each parent and matches against those instead.

13
00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:26.219
Two costs to plan for.

14
00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:33.325
Parents are longer, so each retrieved hit spends more of the prompt budget; retrieve fewer parents than you would children.

15
00:01:33.605 --> 00:01:41.584
And you now maintain two levels, so re-ingesting a document must update parents and children together (lesson 6 covers deletes).

16
00:01:41.864 --> 00:01:51.037
A cheaper relative is to retrieve a chunk plus its immediate neighbors by chunk index, which the offsets and chunk indexes in your metadata make easy.
